          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "Based on the retrieved COPD guidance, an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) is considered mainly for people with a significant history of flare-ups, and only when a blood test marker suggests it may help.\n\n- **Main factor: exacerbation history.** The COPD guideline (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*) says that for people with a significant exacerbation history, dual long-acting bronchodilator therapy is the base, and an ICS is added where the blood eosinophil count is high or where features of coexisting asthma are present. The general COPD reference (*Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)*) similarly says ICS is used \"where indicated by exacerbations or eosinophil count.\"\n- **Why a marker is used:** The guideline says the blood eosinophil count predicts who benefits from an ICS. It is not applied to everyone because ICS use in this group carries an increased risk of pneumonia, so the benefit needs to be identifiable.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give the specific eosinophil threshold or the full list of criteria, because the guideline excerpt starts partway through that section. The asthma guidance also came up in the search, but it covers asthma, not COPD, so I have not used it here. Whether an ICS is appropriate for a particular person is a decision for their clinician, who can weigh their history, test results and risks.\n\nIf you have breathing symptoms that are getting worse quickly, or you are short of breath at rest, seek urgent medical care now rather than relying on this summary.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
